<p>
You know how people have little &ldquo;eternal quests&rdquo; like searching for the perfect wallet, or the perfect t-shirt, or whatever? One of mine is the perfect app for making diagrams in Emacs and Org-mode. My go to solution is <a href="https://graphviz.org/">Graphviz</a>, which I always seem to end up using. It&rsquo;s powerful and not <i>too</i> hard to use but I don&rsquo;t use it enough to internalize its syntax. So I&rsquo;m always on the lookout for a better way. I&rsquo;ve tried a bunch of them but I always go back to Graphviz.
</p>
<p>
I just saw <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SsC-OsHR9RY">this video</a> by Skybert Hacks (Torstein Krause Johansen) about using <a href="https://d2lang.com/">D2</a> for this very problem. Its syntax seems reminiscent of Graphviz but a little simpler and easier to remember. It integrates beautifully with Org-mode so it&rsquo;s just perfect for writing reports, papers, notes, or blog posts.
</p>
<p>
D2 produces nice diagrams—see the Skybert Hacks&rsquo; video for some examples—and is worth trying out. I&rsquo;m going to do just that the next time I need to embed some videos in one of my documents. I just saw the video so I&rsquo;m not familiar with the ins and outs of the app yet but it seems worth a little effort to learn. Once I try it out, I&rsquo;ll report back and let you know how it worked out.</p>
